-- | The elaboration kernel: the mutually-recursive heart of type checking.
--
-- This is the part of the checker that genuinely cannot be split further by
-- moving whole definitions around. Its definitions form one dependency cycle
-- of ~416 functions, held together by a dozen hubs — 'infer', 'check',
-- 'unifyWith' and the implicit-resolution entry points — that call each other
-- through nearly every syntactic form. Breaking it apart would need @.hs-boot@
-- seams, not more modules.
--
-- The organizing idea is __bidirectional__ typing (background:
-- docs\/CONCEPTS.md):
--
--   * 'infer' — synthesize a term's type from the term itself;
--   * 'check' — verify a term against a type the context already expects.
--
-- They call each other, with 'unify' settling metavariables when an inferred
-- type must match an expected one. Both thread a 'Ctx' through @CheckM@.
--
-- Implicit arguments are inserted per the application-spine rules
-- (§16.1.7.1); trait goals follow the §16.3.3 ladder: local implicit context,
-- then global instance search (§14.3.1, with a unique-candidate coherence
-- rule), then boolean-proposition normalization for @(lhs = rhs)@ goals
-- decided by conversion.
--
-- Deliberate v1 restrictions surface as @E_UNSUPPORTED@ diagnostics and are
-- catalogued in SPEC_COMPLIANCE.md; approximations (quantity usage checking,
-- termination) are catalogued in docs\/notes\/IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md.
--
-- Everything layered /below/ this lives in the sibling @Kappa.Check.*@
-- modules; the declaration and module driver layered /above/ it is
-- "Kappa.Check" (Spec §30.1; expressions §16, patterns §17, traits §14,
-- literals §6.1.5, do blocks §18).
module Kappa.Check.Kernel
